Done. The recall path had a mechanical cause of death, and I found the corpse exactly where the task said it was.

What was wrong — `parse_git_log_name_only split commits on blank lines. Real git log --oneline --name-only emits none (verified with cat -A` on this repo's own log). So:
• every multi-commit log collapsed into one entry → commit_count was permanently "1"
• classify_broke_files only ever saw the first commit's message → the red branch was unreachable unless commit #1 happened to say "fix". That's why all 18 validation events are green.
• and when it did fire, every following commit's subject line was recorded as a broken file path.

The fix (src/commands_risk.rs): a small pure helper looks_like_oneline_commit_header — 7–40 hex chars, one space, non-empty subject — flushes the current entry and starts a new one. Blank-line flushing kept, so both existing fixtures pass unchanged. One parser, four call sites repaired.

Tests written first (all red before the fix, 5 new):
1. verbatim 3-commit real-git fixture → entries.len() == 3, files are only real paths, no header leaks
2. commit count is honest
3. the killer: revert as commit #2 now yields exactly {src/foo.rs} — previously {}
4. near-miss side (Day 122): deadbeef.rs, src/abcdef1.rs, `abc123  (short hash), abcdefg1 ` (non-hex), bare hash with no subject all stay files
5. hash-like filename + mid-line hash in a subject

cargo fmt --check, clippy -D warnings, build, test (4487 passed) all green. Committed as faeb5fa8.

**What I did NOT claim.* CLAUDE.md says the red branch is reachable* — not that recall is graded. No failure day has happened since the fix; the number still has to earn itself. And I left `classify_broke_files's contains("fix")` alone as instructed, with a doc comment naming the granularity bug (matches "prefix", "suffix", "fixture", every eval-fix harness commit) so the next task in this arc is discoverable from the code, not just from my memory.

This is the Day 143 lesson landing in the organ my dream depends on: the mechanism was fully built and carrying no traffic. I'd been reading a green meter and concluding I was safe.
